WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.275
TLS commonly supports three goals: confidentiality, integrity, and
endpoint authentication for data in transit.

2
00:00:06.275 --> 00:00:10.630
Confidentiality helps keep network observers from reading protected
traffic.

3
00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:15.368
Integrity helps detect certain kinds of tampering while data moves between
endpoints.

4
00:00:15.368 --> 00:00:24.974
Endpoint authentication helps a client verify that it is communicating
with the expected service identity, and in some designs helps a server
verify the client identity as well.

5
00:00:24.974 --> 00:00:29.264
TLS trust boundaries appear wherever communication crosses between
systems.

6
00:00:29.264 --> 00:00:33.106
A browser connecting to a Python web application crosses one boundary.

7
00:00:33.106 --> 00:00:36.308
A Python service calling another service crosses another.

8
00:00:36.308 --> 00:00:40.022
A worker connecting to a database or message broker crosses another.

9
00:00:40.022 --> 00:00:44.056
Internal versus external location matters, but it is not the same as
trust.

10
00:00:44.056 --> 00:00:51.356
A private network can reduce exposure, yet the identity of the endpoint
still matters when sensitive data or credentials are involved.

11
00:00:51.356 --> 00:00:57.952
Real architectures often include reverse proxies, load balancers,
gateways, service meshes, and application servers.

12
00:00:57.952 --> 00:01:04.547
TLS may terminate at an edge component, continue to a backend service, or
be re-established between internal components.

13
00:01:04.547 --> 00:01:16.842
Teams should know where TLS starts and ends, which component owns
certificates and keys, which headers carry original request information,
and whether backend traffic still needs transport protection based on the
risk model.

14
00:01:16.842 --> 00:01:24.590
TLS should be considered alongside authentication, authorization, logging,
monitoring, deployment architecture, and incident response.

15
00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:34.323
It protects the transport path, but it does not decide whether a user is
allowed to access a record, whether a webhook payload is fresh, or whether
an internal service is trustworthy.

16
00:01:34.323 --> 00:01:37.653
Internal network location alone should not be proof of trust.

17
00:01:37.653 --> 00:01:42.840
Secure systems combine validated transport with clear application and
operational controls.
